1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,101
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the part that's missing, in other words.

2
00:00:04,341 --> 00:00:18,834
[SPEAKER_05]: You have the information, you know that you've read about the Four Noble Truths, you have some recognition of the three characteristics, whichever aspect of the teaching you might want to

3
00:00:19,935 --> 00:00:24,036
[SPEAKER_05]: To focus on, but you're not living in according to it, in accordance with it.

4
00:00:24,397 --> 00:00:25,097
[SPEAKER_05]: You still live.

5
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:33,700
[SPEAKER_05]: And it says, inside of a sotāpanna, everything that is liable to arise, is liable to cease.

6
00:00:57,992 --> 00:01:02,273
[SPEAKER_05]: So, some people might have heard of this parable.

7
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:13,637
[SPEAKER_05]: I think it's from the Hindu tradition that says that what you think is a snake is actually a rope.

8
00:01:15,232 --> 00:01:22,878
[SPEAKER_05]: Therefore, your suffering, on account of that snake, is illusory.

9
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:29,564
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a mistake, because you're deluded.

10
00:01:29,704 --> 00:01:31,866
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't know the ultimate reality.

11
00:01:34,136 --> 00:01:48,422
[SPEAKER_05]: And if you investigate closely, you would realize that that view was implied in most of the modern understandings of what wisdom is.

12
00:01:48,462 --> 00:01:57,525
[SPEAKER_05]: People expect to gain some sort of access to some ultimate hidden reality as a result of their meditation practice.

13
00:01:57,925 --> 00:01:59,346
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, there might be different

14
00:02:00,112 --> 00:02:10,100
[SPEAKER_05]: The point is you conceive the solution to this problem of suffering your life to be this higher reality that you still haven't accessed.

15
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:10,300
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

16
00:02:31,793 --> 00:02:34,798
[SPEAKER_05]: Is there a problem with that?

17
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:39,526
[SPEAKER_05]: What's the difference between that and what the Buddha was teaching in the suttas?

18
00:02:55,793 --> 00:03:02,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if you're looking for some higher or ultimate reality, you have already turned your back to what is present in front of you.

19
00:03:04,875 --> 00:03:11,057
[SPEAKER_02]: And as a result of that, you will not be able to understand what is actually already taking place just now.

20
00:03:12,577 --> 00:03:19,018
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how are you going to understand the origin of your present suffering, if you keep turning your back towards it?

21
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:20,679
[SPEAKER_05]: But what if...

22
00:03:22,118 --> 00:03:31,743
[SPEAKER_05]: What if somebody might say, but the things that are present here are irrelevant, because they're not real, so why should I concern myself with them?

23
00:03:33,884 --> 00:03:35,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's about a pernicious view.

24
00:03:36,625 --> 00:03:36,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

25
00:03:37,886 --> 00:03:38,206
[SPEAKER_03]: But why?

26
00:03:39,927 --> 00:03:42,088
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it might also be, maybe not that they're...

27
00:03:43,209 --> 00:03:49,092
[SPEAKER_03]: It might be said that they're, maybe, not just that they're not relevant, but like, you have a distorted perception of them.

28
00:03:49,888 --> 00:03:55,554
[SPEAKER_03]: Like you have dust in your eyes or something, so that we can't trust your perceptions.

29
00:03:58,357 --> 00:04:06,726
[SPEAKER_02]: But you see, to say that things are not real, you're still talking about things.

30
00:04:07,647 --> 00:04:09,530
[SPEAKER_02]: So the things have to be there first.

31
00:04:10,817 --> 00:04:15,318
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you regard them as not real, as opposed to real.

32
00:04:15,998 --> 00:04:19,919
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's always of things that you say that they are not real.

33
00:04:20,799 --> 00:04:24,600
[SPEAKER_02]: So that position actually in and of itself already doesn't add up.

34
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:28,841
[SPEAKER_02]: Something might be illusory, but it doesn't mean that it isn't there.

35
00:04:30,061 --> 00:04:31,601
[SPEAKER_02]: As an illusion, it is still real.

36
00:04:32,421 --> 00:04:34,562
[SPEAKER_02]: And as such, you suffer on account of that.

37
00:04:35,322 --> 00:04:38,703
[SPEAKER_02]: And that suffering is not because those things are illusory.

38
00:04:40,604 --> 00:04:46,449
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, in other words, where the things are illusory or not, is secondary to the fact that you're suffering.

39
00:04:46,629 --> 00:04:47,430
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point, yeah.

40
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:54,696
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... the thing is, it might factually give you a sense of release from your suffering.

41
00:04:55,397 --> 00:05:02,226
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's for the wrong reason, because, like you just said, the things were never the problem to begin with.

42
00:05:02,767 --> 00:05:10,237
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's kind of the... like, it goes very deeply, and it's not just a...

43
00:05:12,798 --> 00:05:17,201
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not just a coarse view of, again, ultimate reality.

44
00:05:18,382 --> 00:05:21,584
[SPEAKER_05]: It's the view of management, in the most general sense.

45
00:05:22,484 --> 00:05:39,615
[SPEAKER_05]: Often, when people have interpersonal problems, or anger against someone, or something like that, they would think the solution is to reinterpret your relationship with that person.

46
00:05:39,856 --> 00:05:41,497
[SPEAKER_05]: So, tell yourself

47
00:05:42,397 --> 00:05:49,486
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that's called in psychology cognitive reframing, which obviously you don't need the Buddha for.

48
00:05:49,526 --> 00:05:53,011
[SPEAKER_05]: Just telling yourself that, you know, this person is whatever.

49
00:05:53,431 --> 00:05:54,833
[SPEAKER_05]: They're doing this to you because...

50
00:05:56,015 --> 00:06:11,266
[SPEAKER_05]: Their childhood was kind of problematic, or maybe they're having a bad day, and through kind of reframing the situation in that sense, you, quote-unquote, escape the suffering.

51
00:06:11,647 --> 00:06:21,534
[SPEAKER_05]: Alternatively, you might escape the suffering by telling yourself, oh, well, all of these are just... all of these situations are just constantly changing processes.

52
00:06:22,394 --> 00:06:23,015
[SPEAKER_05]: There's nothing

53
00:06:23,721 --> 00:06:25,662
[SPEAKER_05]: There's no inherent essence in this.

54
00:06:26,443 --> 00:06:33,406
[SPEAKER_05]: And through kind of repeating that view to yourself, you eventually kind of stop feeling that which was bothering you.

55
00:06:33,526 --> 00:06:39,870
[SPEAKER_05]: But the thing is, as the Buddha said, what is the origin of suffering?

56
00:06:42,471 --> 00:06:48,735
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it the fact that you didn't have the access to this, to these perceptions, desired perceptions?

57
00:06:49,555 --> 00:06:50,256
[SPEAKER_05]: Is that what he said?

58
00:06:52,926 --> 00:06:53,867
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, obviously not.

59
00:06:53,967 --> 00:06:54,227
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

60
00:06:54,587 --> 00:06:57,309
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's... he said it's craving that's the origin of suffering.

61
00:06:58,029 --> 00:06:59,990
[SPEAKER_05]: So, when is craving there?

62
00:07:04,453 --> 00:07:05,333
[SPEAKER_03]: When is craving there?

63
00:07:05,353 --> 00:07:05,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

64
00:07:07,675 --> 00:07:10,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, well, craving will always be in relation to something.

65
00:07:10,436 --> 00:07:10,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Mm-hmm.

66
00:07:11,017 --> 00:07:14,319
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, whether the thing is now real or illusory, all that, it doesn't matter.

67
00:07:14,379 --> 00:07:14,559
[SPEAKER_05]: Mm-hmm.

68
00:07:14,639 --> 00:07:17,480
[SPEAKER_05]: But more specifically, in relation to what is there craving?

69
00:07:17,821 --> 00:07:18,541
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, I would say feeling.

70
00:07:18,761 --> 00:07:18,961
[SPEAKER_05]: Mm-hmm.

71
00:07:19,201 --> 00:07:19,722
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

72
00:07:19,762 --> 00:07:19,962
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

73
00:07:21,176 --> 00:07:33,903
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, the problem is that when things are unpleasant, in other words, when you haven't still managed to reframe the thing so that you don't feel the pain anymore, you're liable to craving.

74
00:07:35,564 --> 00:07:50,333
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you have to then re-explain and reframe the thing so that it's not felt as unpleasant anymore, that's like dealing with the secondary result of

75
00:07:51,255 --> 00:08:05,240
[SPEAKER_05]: The symptom, basically, of the illness, rather than the illness itself, which is the fact that when things are unpleasant, it doesn't matter whether they're illusory or not illusory or whatever, your mind is still liable to resist them.

76
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,421
[SPEAKER_05]: Or, when they're pleasant, it's still liable to pray for them.

77
00:08:09,361 --> 00:08:15,803
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, you keep assuming that the problem is with the things in the world or with other people.

78
00:08:16,623 --> 00:08:18,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, actually, it isn't.

79
00:08:19,065 --> 00:08:21,406
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's in general sense.

80
00:08:21,446 --> 00:08:32,393
[SPEAKER_05]: So, how then, what is it that you would have to do to free yourself from suffering?

81
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:46,481
[SPEAKER_05]: If you don't have to prove to yourself that things are not real, clearly there is still some work to be done and there is still understanding to be developed.

82
00:08:46,801 --> 00:08:48,002
[SPEAKER_05]: So, how could we kind of

83
00:08:50,317 --> 00:09:08,565
[SPEAKER_05]: Paint a clear picture of what that understanding is, if it has nothing to do with the things in denying the resistance or saying that there's some hidden kind of perceptual distortion on the level of the contents of those things.

84
00:09:12,126 --> 00:09:13,927
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it that you don't understand, in other words?

85
00:09:32,437 --> 00:09:47,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so when the things in the world are allowed to be there, whether it is other people, just whatever, the things of the world, you're not denying that they're there, you're not rationalizing them away by saying it's an illusion, it's a dream, and so on.

86
00:09:47,208 --> 00:09:48,969
[SPEAKER_02]: You just say they are there.

87
00:09:50,130 --> 00:09:53,552
[SPEAKER_02]: You still notice that there is suffering, and now you are asking

88
00:09:55,404 --> 00:09:58,047
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the right way for that suffering to cease?

89
00:09:58,507 --> 00:10:07,375
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so, but specifically in regard to understanding, because, of course, the whole idea is based on the fact that there is ignorance.

90
00:10:07,815 --> 00:10:09,676
[SPEAKER_05]: So, we're not denying that there's ignorance.

91
00:10:09,757 --> 00:10:12,339
[SPEAKER_05]: We're not saying that there's nothing to be attained or something like that.

92
00:10:13,099 --> 00:10:17,043
[SPEAKER_05]: There is a vast lack of understanding there.

93
00:10:17,783 --> 00:10:26,532
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's not on the level of, like, again, pretty much mystical information that you haven't gained.

94
00:10:27,153 --> 00:10:29,455
[SPEAKER_05]: And that if you were to suddenly... That's another wrong view.

95
00:10:29,475 --> 00:10:31,257
[SPEAKER_05]: That, you know, if you... if you...

96
00:10:32,522 --> 00:10:38,563
[SPEAKER_05]: Magically, if you were to kind of get a glimpse of this, all your problems would be immediately resolved.

97
00:10:39,063 --> 00:10:45,704
[SPEAKER_05]: Whereas, the Buddha Sutras clearly say there is no such thing as sudden penetration to knowledge.

98
00:10:45,764 --> 00:10:46,544
[SPEAKER_05]: It's always gradual.

99
00:10:46,764 --> 00:10:47,004
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

100
00:10:47,785 --> 00:10:50,505
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is that understanding that's missing?

101
00:10:51,445 --> 00:10:56,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, can I just kind of butt in with another thought before we try to answer that question?

102
00:10:57,426 --> 00:11:01,287
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess, existentially speaking, even the notion that

103
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:04,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is false.

104
00:11:04,381 --> 00:11:05,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, everything is an illusion.

105
00:11:07,322 --> 00:11:09,903
[SPEAKER_01]: I think this is sort of what Ajahn Pascoe is saying.

106
00:11:10,283 --> 00:11:15,886
[SPEAKER_01]: But, um, that is still blaming those things.

107
00:11:15,926 --> 00:11:18,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of offloading the understanding.

108
00:11:18,047 --> 00:11:19,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, I mean, existentially speaking,

109
00:11:23,146 --> 00:11:25,927
[SPEAKER_01]: The appearance of things still belongs to those things.

110
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:36,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your understanding of understanding depends on these things being otherwise, then that's not really in your control.

111
00:11:37,411 --> 00:11:39,511
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, and for that matter, it's also infinite.

112
00:11:40,132 --> 00:11:41,112
[SPEAKER_05]: Because how do you know

113
00:11:41,764 --> 00:11:46,727
[SPEAKER_05]: That there isn't a more ultimate reality than the one that you're... Yeah.

114
00:11:47,047 --> 00:11:55,553
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's... you're just chasing this kind of... you're chasing this thing that you yourself are creating, in other words.

115
00:11:56,974 --> 00:11:57,975
[SPEAKER_05]: And, so...

116
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:20,570
[SPEAKER_05]: And, I mean, there's many angles you could approach it, but also the suttas would say that, you know, whether it's past, present, future, inferior, superior, coarse or fine, regardless of what it is, all form, all feeling, all perception,

117
00:12:21,422 --> 00:12:25,544
[SPEAKER_05]: All activities, all consciousness must be seen in the same way.

118
00:12:25,904 --> 00:12:27,325
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, there's nothing special.

119
00:12:28,226 --> 00:12:35,329
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if your view is that by finding something special, you free yourself from suffering.

120
00:12:36,130 --> 00:12:45,575
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, as much as you might want to deny that, you might come up with all these mystical views that, oh, look, this thing that I discovered is outside of the five aggregates.

121
00:12:47,898 --> 00:12:50,080
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why it's, quote-unquote, the escape.

122
00:12:50,841 --> 00:12:57,588
[SPEAKER_05]: But all you're doing there is you're failing to apply that blanket recognition that all forms

123
00:12:57,884 --> 00:12:59,505
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it doesn't matter how you spin it.

124
00:12:59,545 --> 00:13:05,448
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, anything that is arisen has to be within those five aggregates.

125
00:13:05,688 --> 00:13:09,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, which includes the idea that there might be something outside of the aggregates.

126
00:13:09,790 --> 00:13:24,858
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter what perception it is, what kind of... Yeah, like the most exalted sense of other-worldly kind of sanctuary from everything.

127
00:13:26,108 --> 00:13:26,788
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it's felt.

128
00:13:27,068 --> 00:13:30,510
[SPEAKER_05]: You can already see that it's felt, because clearly you regard it as pleasant.

129
00:13:30,530 --> 00:13:33,271
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's already within the five areas.

130
00:13:33,291 --> 00:13:34,892
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter what you think.

131
00:13:35,072 --> 00:13:43,195
[SPEAKER_02]: So, ideas or ideals that you are striving after, that is also, right, mental, but it's fully within the aggregates.

132
00:13:44,636 --> 00:13:45,797
[SPEAKER_05]: Even the most, eh...

133
00:13:46,986 --> 00:13:52,748
[SPEAKER_05]: acute experience that you might get at the end of your meditation, it doesn't matter.

134
00:13:53,008 --> 00:13:56,450
[SPEAKER_05]: It has to be form, feeling, perception, intention, and consciousness.

135
00:13:56,470 --> 00:13:58,611
[SPEAKER_05]: You're completely confined within those things.

136
00:13:58,671 --> 00:13:58,851
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

137
00:14:00,615 --> 00:14:16,105
[SPEAKER_02]: That means that your meditation practice ironically becomes quite real when you adopt that view, but also, in a certain sense, a bit boring, because now all your notions of what is esoteric and special and mystical are going to be within those aggregates.

138
00:14:16,565 --> 00:14:16,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

139
00:14:16,965 --> 00:14:17,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Right?

140
00:14:17,205 --> 00:14:19,267
[SPEAKER_02]: So it just sort of flattens out.

141
00:14:19,287 --> 00:14:26,451
[SPEAKER_02]: So one needs to be prepared that it might actually be somewhat grey, dull and boring, but ironically, that's far more real.

142
00:14:26,851 --> 00:14:27,211
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

143
00:14:27,271 --> 00:14:27,452
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

144
00:14:27,592 --> 00:14:27,872
[SPEAKER_05]: Because

145
00:14:29,308 --> 00:14:30,669
[SPEAKER_05]: Did you suffer?

146
00:14:30,889 --> 00:14:33,670
[SPEAKER_05]: What was the thing that you were suffering about?

147
00:14:34,851 --> 00:14:36,012
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the ultimate reality?

148
00:14:36,852 --> 00:14:56,803
[SPEAKER_05]: Or was it the mundane kind of pleasure and pain when people were, you know, not doing... as Suttas would often say, when you were separated from things that were dear, united with things that were not dear, sickness, aging, death, bodily displeasure, mental displeasure,

