[SPEAKER_05]: It's as simple as that.

180
00:18:38,972 --> 00:18:49,800
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not being sufficiently clear about the fact that what you regard as pleasant, it's real as such.

181
00:18:49,900 --> 00:18:59,607
[SPEAKER_05]: There is a pleasant aspect to it, but it comes with this kind of simultaneous danger, peril.

182
00:19:00,419 --> 00:19:00,980
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, eh...

183
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:09,507
[SPEAKER_05]: I think the sutta also gave a simile that it's like, eh... like a razor blade that is honey smeared all over it.

184
00:19:09,667 --> 00:19:13,510
[SPEAKER_05]: And you can't help yourself but to keep licking it and cutting yourself at the same time.

185
00:19:15,051 --> 00:19:16,152
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... and that's all.

186
00:19:16,252 --> 00:19:24,619
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you wouldn't need to... Because, see, actually, it's not just you wouldn't need to go and chase after the ultimate reality.

187
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:27,722
[SPEAKER_05]: You realize the ultimate reality

188
00:19:28,232 --> 00:19:29,252
[SPEAKER_05]: Isn't ultimate at all.

189
00:19:29,532 --> 00:19:53,658
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you are kind of defining it as such, because of your assumption, but it still is, as I said before, it still is pleasant, and there is still a gratification there, there is still a danger there, and there is still an escape there, but because you kind of stopped at the content of how special it is, you didn't realize that actually it had to be included within the same exact principle.

190
00:19:57,443 --> 00:19:57,984
[SPEAKER_05]: So, eh...

191
00:19:58,344 --> 00:19:59,626
[SPEAKER_05]: So, appearance is our view.

192
00:20:00,126 --> 00:20:00,507
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

193
00:20:00,527 --> 00:20:01,448
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's all you know.

194
00:20:01,488 --> 00:20:01,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

195
00:20:01,688 --> 00:20:04,632
[SPEAKER_02]: And in that sense, all there is, is what appears.

196
00:20:04,772 --> 00:20:04,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

197
00:20:05,093 --> 00:20:05,673
[SPEAKER_05]: There's...

198
00:20:05,773 --> 00:20:08,136
[SPEAKER_05]: Appearance is reality, to an extent.

199
00:20:08,417 --> 00:20:08,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

200
00:20:12,529 --> 00:20:23,694
[SPEAKER_05]: And actually, speaking about that, there's a sutta where the Buddha kind of alludes to this whole view that things are quote-unquote not real.

201
00:20:24,835 --> 00:20:29,717
[SPEAKER_05]: It's Sankhya 2294.

202
00:20:29,837 --> 00:20:36,540
[SPEAKER_05]: It says, because I do not argue with the world, it is the world that argues with me.

203
00:20:37,701 --> 00:20:41,463
[SPEAKER_05]: One whose speech is in line with the dhamma does not argue with anyone in the world.

204
00:20:42,123 --> 00:20:45,904
[SPEAKER_05]: What the wise deem as not existing, I too say does not exist.

205
00:20:46,665 --> 00:20:49,265
[SPEAKER_05]: What the wise deem as existing, I too say exists.

206
00:20:50,086 --> 00:20:54,447
[SPEAKER_05]: And what do the wise deem as not existing, which I too say does not exist.

207
00:20:55,447 --> 00:20:59,849
[SPEAKER_05]: Form that is permanent, everlasting, eternal, and imperishable.

208
00:21:00,569 --> 00:21:06,311
[SPEAKER_05]: Feeling, perception, activities, consciousness, that is permanent, everlasting, eternal, and imperishable.

209
00:21:06,571 --> 00:21:10,033
[SPEAKER_05]: This is what the wise deem as not existing, which I too say does not exist.

210
00:21:10,993 --> 00:21:14,334
[SPEAKER_05]: And what do the wise deem as existing, which I too say exists?

211
00:21:15,195 --> 00:21:18,276
[SPEAKER_05]: Form that is impermanent, suffering, imperishable.

212
00:21:18,576 --> 00:21:20,837
[SPEAKER_05]: Emphasis on it exists.

213
00:21:21,617 --> 00:21:23,238
[SPEAKER_05]: Feeling, perception, choices.

214
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:38,742
[SPEAKER_05]: There exists a mundane phenomenon in the world, and that is what the Tathāgata awakens to and comprehends.

215
00:21:39,462 --> 00:21:48,844
[SPEAKER_05]: Having awakened to it and comprehended it, he teaches, asserts, establishes, clarifies, analyzes and reveals it.

216
00:21:49,044 --> 00:21:51,085
[SPEAKER_05]: And what is that mundane phenomenon in the world?

217
00:21:51,977 --> 00:21:56,479
[SPEAKER_05]: Form is a mundane phenomenon in the world, feeling, perception, activities.

218
00:21:57,320 --> 00:22:08,546
[SPEAKER_05]: Consciousness is a mundane phenomenon in the world that the Tathāgata awakens to and comprehends, then he explains, teaches, asserts, establishes, clarifies, analyzes, and reveals it.

219
00:22:09,847 --> 00:22:17,731
[SPEAKER_05]: This being so, what can I do about a foolish, ordinary person, blind and sightless, who does not know and see?

220
00:22:19,179 --> 00:22:26,682
[SPEAKER_05]: Suppose, in other words, somebody who thinks that things don't exist, even though he's clearly saying that there are things that exist.

221
00:22:26,802 --> 00:22:27,042
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

222
00:22:27,562 --> 00:22:32,224
[SPEAKER_05]: Suppose there was a blue water lily or a pink or white lotus.

223
00:22:33,044 --> 00:22:38,246
[SPEAKER_05]: Though it sprouted and grew in the water, it will rise up above the water and stand with no water clinging to it.

224
00:22:38,826 --> 00:22:47,950
[SPEAKER_05]: In the same way, though the Tathāgata has been born and has grown up in the world, he lives having overcome the world, unsullied by the world.

225
00:22:50,627 --> 00:22:55,951
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that simile almost says it all.

226
00:22:56,432 --> 00:23:01,696
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, what is it that makes you be...

227
00:23:02,977 --> 00:23:11,303
[SPEAKER_05]: So, given that the mundane things exist, now that they don't, what is it that makes you get sullied by them?

228
00:23:11,523 --> 00:23:18,889
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it that makes the lotus become sullied by the water, by the muddy water, yeah?

229
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:22,921
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it the things themselves?

230
00:23:29,768 --> 00:23:34,033
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, like you were saying before, passion towards people, desire and lust.

231
00:23:35,635 --> 00:23:35,855
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

232
00:23:37,316 --> 00:23:40,079
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's that simple, really.

233
00:23:40,099 --> 00:23:40,920
[SPEAKER_05]: Not understanding

234
00:23:42,046 --> 00:23:47,689
[SPEAKER_05]: That things are not worthy of passion, you have passion towards them.

235
00:23:47,849 --> 00:23:50,971
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the kind of beginningless state, as the Buddha said.

236
00:23:51,131 --> 00:23:54,253
[SPEAKER_05]: You cannot find a reason why passion is there.

237
00:23:54,353 --> 00:23:57,955
[SPEAKER_05]: Just wherever you look, it's... it's gonna... you're gonna find that it's there already.

238
00:23:58,135 --> 00:24:00,256
[SPEAKER_05]: You didn't create it.

239
00:24:00,396 --> 00:24:09,421
[SPEAKER_05]: As a matter of choice, it's the fact that you have indirectly kind of generated it through your choices of rooted in passion.

240
00:24:10,282 --> 00:24:10,582
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

241
00:24:13,518 --> 00:24:15,178
[SPEAKER_05]: That's something that many people would understand.

242
00:24:15,558 --> 00:24:17,339
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you know, passion.

243
00:24:17,479 --> 00:24:21,759
[SPEAKER_05]: The Buddha talks about giving up passion, aversion and delusion.

244
00:24:21,879 --> 00:24:25,540
[SPEAKER_05]: And he says that nibbāna is the cessation of passion, aversion and delusion.

245
00:24:27,200 --> 00:24:29,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Now... Yeah.

246
00:24:29,661 --> 00:24:31,841
[SPEAKER_02]: So, just to pause here now for a minute.

247
00:24:31,861 --> 00:24:40,423
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you become attached to material things in the world, then you are giving power to things that are inherently powerless.

248
00:24:41,603 --> 00:24:42,923
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can't now be without them.

249
00:24:45,253 --> 00:24:45,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Right?

250
00:24:45,933 --> 00:24:50,156
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the foolishness of becoming attached to things in the world.

251
00:24:50,797 --> 00:24:56,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, seeing that you are stuck, you want to deny the reality of those things by saying that they are illusory.

252
00:24:56,660 --> 00:25:01,623
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, actually, it is due to the wrong choices that you allowed yourself to become attached to them.

253
00:25:01,904 --> 00:25:02,544
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the issue.

254
00:25:03,004 --> 00:25:05,646
[SPEAKER_02]: And our amount of denying the reality of those things

255
00:25:07,720 --> 00:25:08,460
[SPEAKER_02]: That causes a problem.

256
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:08,780
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

257
00:25:08,900 --> 00:25:12,181
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you make the house dirty and your solution is to demolish the whole thing.

258
00:25:12,821 --> 00:25:13,941
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

259
00:25:14,742 --> 00:25:17,702
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's quicker, of course.

260
00:25:19,723 --> 00:25:33,485
[SPEAKER_05]: But then, given that people know that, again, passion, aversion and delusion, that's like basic Buddhist dogma and, you know, has to be abandoned, and nibbāna is a cessation of those three things.

261
00:25:34,646 --> 00:25:35,706
[SPEAKER_05]: Almost everybody knows that.

262
00:25:37,499 --> 00:25:44,224
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if we're saying, well, that's what wisdom is, then somebody might say, well, I already know that, but I'm not enlightened yet.

263
00:25:44,444 --> 00:25:46,766
[SPEAKER_05]: So, why is that?

264
00:25:46,866 --> 00:25:48,107
[SPEAKER_05]: What is there that's missing?

265
00:25:55,033 --> 00:25:56,193
[SPEAKER_04]: The cessation of those things.

266
00:25:57,575 --> 00:25:59,436
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but...

267
00:26:00,417 --> 00:26:04,000
[SPEAKER_05]: So, basically, let's say, how would they bring about the cessation of those things?

268
00:26:04,852 --> 00:26:29,472
[SPEAKER_05]: If it's not through accessing some ultimate reality, if you kind of cannot help but be exposed to the same old, mundane, boring form, feeling, perception, intention, consciousness, then how can you, as the Sutta says, how can you kind of rise above all of that without then having to step into an alternate reality?

269
00:26:33,821 --> 00:26:42,026
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so, I mean, it's...

270
00:26:43,087 --> 00:26:47,790
[SPEAKER_05]: I think we'll probably repeat it in every single talk, but it's...

271
00:26:50,032 --> 00:26:59,499
[SPEAKER_05]: The reason why the same knowledges and kind of, to some extent, intellectual information that you have,

272
00:27:01,008 --> 00:27:06,641
[SPEAKER_05]: is not seen in such a way that it would free you from suffering, is because, yes, you know in principle that...

273
00:27:07,530 --> 00:27:10,491
[SPEAKER_05]: Form is impermanent, suffering, not-self.

274
00:27:11,371 --> 00:27:14,192
[SPEAKER_05]: But do you live as if that's the case?

275
00:27:14,512 --> 00:27:24,974
[SPEAKER_05]: Or do you still act like someone who thinks that certain forms are pleasant, are permanent, and are yours?

276
00:27:26,074 --> 00:27:26,995
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's as simple as that.

277
00:27:27,055 --> 00:27:37,277
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you cannot, at the same time, expect that you will be... that that same information that you have will be sinking in.

278
00:27:38,098 --> 00:27:45,304
[SPEAKER_05]: on the level that it must, for the passion to dissipate, if you continue to do the things that are fully rooted in that same passion.

279
00:27:45,364 --> 00:27:46,645
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's it's that simple.

280
00:27:46,705 --> 00:27:53,971
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, if you were to actually go through, like, that's why when the Buddha would talk about the final discernment, like,

281
00:27:54,812 --> 00:28:08,581
[SPEAKER_05]: To someone, for the first time, he wouldn't start by saying like, oh, yeah, nothing exists, it's impermanent and, you know, you just have to find that hidden reality and all your problems will be solved.

282
00:28:08,702 --> 00:28:09,282
[SPEAKER_05]: He would say, no.

283
00:28:09,882 --> 00:28:14,686
[SPEAKER_05]: First, a clansman hears the teaching, acquires faith,

284
00:28:15,626 --> 00:28:16,446
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, they go forth.

285
00:28:17,287 --> 00:28:32,331
[SPEAKER_05]: They take on the precepts, virtue, sense restraint, gives on the whole description of the reality of training, the work of purifying the mind from unwholesome states, and then, when the mind is sufficiently purified, they understand what?

286
00:28:32,431 --> 00:28:33,532
[SPEAKER_05]: The ultimate reality?

287
00:28:33,572 --> 00:28:33,732
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

288
00:28:33,792 --> 00:28:42,995
[SPEAKER_05]: They understand suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the way that leads to the cessation of suffering.

289
00:28:43,015 --> 00:28:43,675
[SPEAKER_05]: They understand

290
00:28:45,167 --> 00:28:51,129
[SPEAKER_05]: The defilements, the origin of the defilements, the cessation of the defilements, and the way that leads to the cessation of the defilements.

291
00:28:51,929 --> 00:29:04,813
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, once that's done, it says, there's nothing more to be done, being is destroyed, and they abide at ease until the aggregates break apart.

292
00:29:05,453 --> 00:29:05,753
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

293
00:29:08,575 --> 00:29:10,196
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the part that's missing, in other words.

294
00:29:10,456 --> 00:29:24,920
[SPEAKER_05]: You have the information, you know that you've read about the Four Noble Truths, you have some recognition of the three characteristics, whichever aspect of the teaching you might want to

295
00:29:26,020 --> 00:29:30,122
[SPEAKER_05]: To focus on, but you're not living in according to it, in accordance with it.

296
00:29:30,482 --> 00:29:39,787
[SPEAKER_05]: You still live, and it says, the insight of a sotāpanna, everything that is liable to arise, is liable to cease.

297
00:29:40,988 --> 00:29:48,432
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, you, again, like, look for some magical experience where you're going to get that insight.

298
00:29:49,479 --> 00:30:03,108
[SPEAKER_05]: But, eh... what you actually have to look at... to see is, am I living in such a way that some things that are liable to arise, am I trying to not make... to make them never cease?

299
00:30:04,433 --> 00:30:11,116
[SPEAKER_05]: Am I acting in such a way that I assume or hope that I could maintain some things?

300
00:30:11,236 --> 00:30:11,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Or don't.

301
00:30:11,876 --> 00:30:12,777
[SPEAKER_05]: You want to get rid of that.

302
00:30:12,957 --> 00:30:13,317
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

303
00:30:13,517 --> 00:30:26,643
[SPEAKER_05]: So, because another one is like the whole ties in with this whole view of alternate reality and hidden perceptions is like the flux, like the constant change.

304
00:30:27,403 --> 00:30:30,305
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, when you have something that makes you feel pain, you

305
00:30:31,225 --> 00:30:35,909
[SPEAKER_05]: You conceive of the solution as being to tell yourself, oh, this will pass.

306
00:30:36,890 --> 00:30:37,671
[SPEAKER_05]: This too shall pass.

307
00:30:37,691 --> 00:30:38,452
[SPEAKER_05]: This is impermanent.

308
00:30:38,572 --> 00:30:39,753
[SPEAKER_05]: It's all constantly changing.

309
00:30:39,793 --> 00:30:43,136
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just molecules coming and going.

310
00:30:43,616 --> 00:30:44,437
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't... Yeah.

311
00:30:44,737 --> 00:30:53,665
[SPEAKER_02]: So, either you deny the reality of it by saying it's constantly changing, therefore there's no essence, and it's all an illusion, failing to see that you actually still suffer.

312
00:30:54,266 --> 00:30:55,527
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's slightly a contradiction.

313
00:30:56,574 --> 00:31:10,403
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, more accurately, like, if you had actually understood that it's liable to change, then why are you trying to get rid of it through that same, you know, supposedly right insight?

314
00:31:10,443 --> 00:31:12,225
[SPEAKER_05]: But you realize, what is your attitude?

315
00:31:12,445 --> 00:31:14,086
[SPEAKER_05]: What is your implied view there?

316
00:31:14,226 --> 00:31:14,446
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

317
00:31:14,666 --> 00:31:18,169
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, no, no, actually, I'm not sure that it's going to pass away.

318
00:31:18,249 --> 00:31:20,590
[SPEAKER_05]: So that's why I need to kind of... To do something about it.

319
00:31:20,630 --> 00:31:23,072
[SPEAKER_05]: So to that extent, you are not rightly

320
00:31:25,900 --> 00:31:53,632
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, and that's why you try to do any of these things, understand the impermanence, understand suffering, understand not-self, without having developed virtue, without having developed sensual strength, without having developed the prior training, you will be acting out of your single tendencies of impatience, of craving, of trying to get rid of things, and for that very reason alone, the the misconceiving of things as permanent,

321
00:31:54,475 --> 00:31:58,557
[SPEAKER_05]: Misconceiving of craving... of not seeing even your own craving.

322
00:31:58,777 --> 00:31:59,938
[SPEAKER_05]: All of that is still going to be there.

323
00:32:00,138 --> 00:32:03,079
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why that's the that's the prerequisite.

324
00:32:03,239 --> 00:32:05,520
[SPEAKER_05]: Even though it's not the dhamma, there is no dhamma without it.

325
00:32:07,201 --> 00:32:12,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so by... so, due to immense impatience, for example, which is rooted in the view that it will not go away,

326
00:32:13,260 --> 00:32:20,306
[SPEAKER_02]: You actually... you keep reinforcing the view that those things are permanent and that that is where you should do something about it.

327
00:32:20,887 --> 00:32:25,291
[SPEAKER_02]: And as long as that's what you do, you're not gonna see that they are in fact impermanent.

328
00:32:25,471 --> 00:32:25,651
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

329
00:32:25,971 --> 00:32:37,881
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the way to actually come to see things as impermanent, which does not mean that they are constantly changing, is ironically to endure those things which are real and are there enduring.

330
00:32:38,042 --> 00:32:38,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with not-self.

331
00:32:39,203 --> 00:32:39,483
[SPEAKER_05]: If you

332
00:32:40,268 --> 00:32:48,073
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, it's quite amusing that sometimes the not-self idea would be re-explained as like, oh, it's empty of inherent essence.

333
00:32:48,834 --> 00:32:52,976
[SPEAKER_05]: Meaning, like, again, another way of saying it doesn't really exist.

334
00:32:53,737 --> 00:33:00,261
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you're supposed to apply that view to everything you encounter, especially when it makes you suffer.

335
00:33:00,741 --> 00:33:04,664
[SPEAKER_05]: And why is it that you're doing that?

336
00:33:04,804 --> 00:33:06,205
[SPEAKER_05]: Because you're taking it personally.

337
00:33:06,845 --> 00:33:10,207
[SPEAKER_05]: If you wouldn't be taking it personally, it wouldn't be making you suffer in the first place.

338
00:33:10,347 --> 00:33:10,587
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

339
00:33:11,247 --> 00:33:15,009
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you realize this is the level where these things have to be understood.

340
00:33:15,189 --> 00:33:19,011
[SPEAKER_05]: On the level of, as we often say, the intention behind your action.

341
00:33:19,111 --> 00:33:19,991
[SPEAKER_05]: Why am I doing this?

342
00:33:20,972 --> 00:33:25,394
[SPEAKER_05]: That's where you will be holding all these assumptions and views.

343
00:33:28,155 --> 00:33:30,196
[SPEAKER_03]: So...

344
00:33:30,216 --> 00:33:32,657
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, it's something active that you're doing.

345
00:33:32,757 --> 00:33:34,758
[SPEAKER_03]: All these methods are too late.

346
00:33:35,380 --> 00:33:40,161
[SPEAKER_03]: You're already struck by suffering and you try to do something about it.

347
00:33:40,401 --> 00:33:45,103
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, and it will only last for a limited amount of time.

348
00:33:45,163 --> 00:33:54,545
[SPEAKER_05]: Whatever effect you get out of these, out of pasting these views over your experience, will only last a limited amount of time and that's why you need to keep renewing it.

349
00:33:55,540 --> 00:34:11,368
[SPEAKER_05]: If you haven't done the formal meditation, where you access the ultimate reality again for a while, you will quickly find that, you know, you cannot actually over overrun the fact that pleasure and pain do arise.

350
00:34:11,688 --> 00:34:22,994
[SPEAKER_05]: I might tell myself all I want, that it's not real, and there's this other dimension that's more real than it, but, eh... the the the kind of raw fact of

351
00:34:24,787 --> 00:34:29,689
[SPEAKER_05]: Of it not actually caring about you, and what you think about it will reveal itself again.

352
00:34:30,730 --> 00:34:31,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

353
00:34:31,570 --> 00:34:33,911
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you know that the ultimate reality is not more painful?

354
00:34:34,071 --> 00:34:34,272
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

355
00:34:34,952 --> 00:34:43,356
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, ultimately it is, because you realize the simple fact of needing to run away from things is precisely the reason why you're suffering.

356
00:34:43,416 --> 00:34:43,656
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

357
00:34:44,252 --> 00:34:49,713
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's actually... and that's why the Buddha said there is right liberation and there is wrong liberation.

358
00:34:50,353 --> 00:35:09,418
[SPEAKER_05]: So, even though these things might... you might get a sense that they're liberating you from the suffering, through that same liberation, you're inadvertently cultivating the same thing that gave rise to the problems in the end.

359
00:35:09,438 --> 00:35:12,839
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you kind of... it's like a way of...

360
00:35:14,292 --> 00:35:21,595
[SPEAKER_05]: Getting rid of the symptoms, a way that obscures the symptoms, while actually still making the actual illness worse.

361
00:35:22,215 --> 00:35:29,618
[SPEAKER_05]: So then, whenever you stop, um, hiding those symptoms, you will probably find that the illness has actually become worse.

362
00:35:29,858 --> 00:35:41,622
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why... what often happens, like, when you become... when you find safety in these... these kind of higher perceptions or whatever, and... and then you don't get to do it for a while, you will see that

363
00:35:42,318 --> 00:35:49,362
[SPEAKER_05]: Things actually affect you twice as much, because you've been cultivating weakness by running away from it.

364
00:35:50,143 --> 00:35:59,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because why would you restrain yourself and question your greed, hatred and illusion, if you still assume that the solution is finding this ultimate reality?

365
00:35:59,448 --> 00:36:04,612
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a wrong view, but also it prevents you from actually seeing what the real work is.

366
00:36:04,812 --> 00:36:06,353
[SPEAKER_05]: That will also exactly happen.

367
00:36:06,373 --> 00:36:08,494
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, that's why then, yeah, the whole

368
00:36:11,124 --> 00:36:22,798
[SPEAKER_05]: Only for as long as I still haven't achieved this higher reality, and once I do, all of this becomes secondary, because it was never real to begin with.

369
00:36:23,218 --> 00:36:23,539
[SPEAKER_05]: So...

370
00:36:25,113 --> 00:36:27,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what makes it a wrong view.

371
00:36:27,774 --> 00:36:30,856
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that it doesn't lead to freedom from suffering.

372
00:36:30,896 --> 00:36:31,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

373
00:36:31,836 --> 00:36:36,739
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like it's a wrong view because it's false and it also doesn't happen to lead to freedom from suffering.

374
00:36:36,779 --> 00:36:39,860
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the benchmark for... Yeah.

375
00:36:39,880 --> 00:36:41,301
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it doesn't lead...

376
00:36:41,645 --> 00:36:44,166
[SPEAKER_05]: It might lead to removing the causes of suffering.

377
00:36:44,386 --> 00:36:47,868
[SPEAKER_05]: It might remove... that's why I said, like, the symptoms and the illness.

378
00:36:47,908 --> 00:36:56,092
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the symptoms of you actually experiencing pleasant feelings at that time, you can suppress that.

379
00:36:56,493 --> 00:36:58,654
[SPEAKER_05]: But mind you, you don't need the Buddha for that.

380
00:36:58,754 --> 00:37:02,336
[SPEAKER_05]: You can do that in a million different ways.

381
00:37:04,957 --> 00:37:06,898
[SPEAKER_05]: But the cause, which is a craving,

382
00:37:07,870 --> 00:37:12,933
[SPEAKER_05]: The same thing that you're still cultivating toward these higher realities, that's still there.

383
00:37:13,353 --> 00:37:16,575
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just that kind of... it will have a delayed effect.

384
00:37:16,835 --> 00:37:18,376
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it will catch up on you later on.

385
00:37:19,056 --> 00:37:22,218
[SPEAKER_05]: It won't be apparent right then and there.

386
00:37:22,899 --> 00:37:31,484
[SPEAKER_05]: That same attitude that you're cultivating toward these things is what generated the same suffering that you were running away from in the past.

387
00:37:31,944 --> 00:37:33,385
[SPEAKER_05]: It's the same with saṃsāra, actually.

388
00:37:33,585 --> 00:37:35,646
[SPEAKER_05]: In the moment, it feels like it's great.

389
00:37:36,423 --> 00:37:48,850
[SPEAKER_05]: Only afterwards, when you're kind of cut away from that which you were welcoming, you realize the suffering that you've... the liability to suffering that you've been feeding.

390
00:37:49,991 --> 00:38:00,176
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you create that dependence on something that's higher, and now that higher thing becomes your addiction, becomes that which you need to maintain.

391
00:38:00,316 --> 00:38:01,277
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why the Buddha said,

392
00:38:02,657 --> 00:38:07,027
[SPEAKER_05]: The one who gives things up rightly, he does so without taking up something new.

393
00:38:08,878 --> 00:38:22,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because somebody might have an idea of some higher or ultimate reality, and in order to go there, they might in fact abandon a whole bunch of, let's say, other attachments or lower things, but still... For the wrong reason.

394
00:38:23,226 --> 00:38:24,447
[SPEAKER_02]: For the wrong reason.

395
00:38:24,967 --> 00:38:25,908
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they can...

396
00:38:25,928 --> 00:38:28,430
[SPEAKER_05]: Replacing one craving with a more subtle craving.

397
00:38:28,570 --> 00:38:28,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

398
00:38:29,170 --> 00:38:33,813
[SPEAKER_02]: So, but what we are now saying is that all of that giving up still needs to happen,

399
00:38:34,714 --> 00:38:38,039
[SPEAKER_02]: But in the absence of you going somewhere else, basically.

400
00:38:38,079 --> 00:38:46,432
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, and that's what's going to make it, as you said, it's going to make it more boring, because you realize, yeah, I'm not going to get some magical reward out of this.

401
00:38:46,813 --> 00:38:49,437
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just, I will have the same things, but...

402
00:38:50,076 --> 00:38:50,996
[SPEAKER_05]: I won't suffer anymore.

403
00:38:51,016 --> 00:38:52,197
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

404
00:38:52,517 --> 00:38:55,398
[SPEAKER_05]: And that doesn't sound as exhilarating.

405
00:38:55,498 --> 00:38:55,878
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

406
00:38:56,198 --> 00:39:00,140
[SPEAKER_05]: But well, that's that's how life is.

407
00:39:00,180 --> 00:39:08,623
[SPEAKER_05]: It's it's it's the things that are shiny and pleasant and quick fixes are usually just gimmicks.

408
00:39:09,223 --> 00:39:14,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

409
00:39:14,905 --> 00:39:16,206
[SPEAKER_03]: It's much more challenging as well.

410
00:39:17,066 --> 00:39:18,827
[SPEAKER_03]: I could listen to this and think I need to

411
00:39:19,417 --> 00:39:21,298
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to tighten up my restraint.

412
00:39:21,418 --> 00:39:22,858
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to start giving things up.

413
00:39:23,979 --> 00:39:28,520
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to see the danger, not just this gratification.

414
00:39:29,521 --> 00:39:31,802
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I need to see...

415
00:39:33,842 --> 00:39:37,084
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to see a rope as a snake, instead of a snake as a rope.

416
00:39:37,284 --> 00:39:41,425
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, I just replace the beautiful with, like, the ugly.

417
00:39:41,765 --> 00:39:43,486
[SPEAKER_03]: And I just deny that aspect.

418
00:39:48,792 --> 00:39:50,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, thinking I'm practicing mindfulness.

419
00:39:52,214 --> 00:39:54,255
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, actually, that's another thing I wanted to go back to.

420
00:39:54,275 --> 00:40:04,462
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the right type of wisdom, since that's what we started to talk with, is not that the snake, the rope, is not a snake.

421
00:40:06,043 --> 00:40:16,789
[SPEAKER_05]: It's that even when it is a snake, and it's actually a poisonous viper, and it's actually biting you right in the neck, you don't suffer.

422
00:40:18,380 --> 00:40:19,260
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what the wisdom is.

423
00:40:20,020 --> 00:40:22,621
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not that, oh, this is not what it is.

424
00:40:24,962 --> 00:40:25,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

425
00:40:25,942 --> 00:40:29,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that would be the goal of the practice, I think.

426
00:40:29,103 --> 00:40:31,164
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what your understanding is measured by.

427
00:40:31,444 --> 00:40:31,664
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

428
00:40:31,724 --> 00:40:37,906
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not how much you can pretend that things are not what they are, or make them appear otherwise.

429
00:40:38,826 --> 00:40:42,127
[SPEAKER_05]: It's how, when the worst case scenario comes,

430
00:40:43,218 --> 00:40:44,800
[SPEAKER_05]: And you cannot escape from it.

431
00:40:44,820 --> 00:40:45,020
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

432
00:40:45,060 --> 00:40:48,183
[SPEAKER_05]: You cannot go left, right, up or down, front or back.

433
00:40:48,463 --> 00:40:50,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the question is, has the work been done or not?

434
00:40:50,705 --> 00:40:51,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

435
00:40:51,726 --> 00:40:51,966
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

436
00:40:57,731 --> 00:41:01,735
[SPEAKER_05]: And there was one more sutta that I wanted to read, to eh...

437
00:41:06,273 --> 00:41:17,663
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it says that the the eight worldly phenomena, loka-dhamma, so that's another common teaching that appears in the suttas.

438
00:41:17,723 --> 00:41:25,549
[SPEAKER_05]: It says, because the eight worldly phenomena follow the world around, and the world follows the eight worldly phenomena around.

439
00:41:26,330 --> 00:41:26,730
[SPEAKER_05]: What eight?

440
00:41:27,631 --> 00:41:28,392
[SPEAKER_05]: Gain and loss,

441
00:41:30,235 --> 00:41:37,502
[SPEAKER_05]: of ultimate realities, uh, honor and dishonor, blame and praise, pleasure and displeasure.

442
00:41:38,343 --> 00:41:43,247
[SPEAKER_05]: These eight worldly phenomena follow the world around, and the world follows the eight worldly phenomena around.

443
00:41:44,909 --> 00:41:52,456
[SPEAKER_05]: For an unlearned ordinary person, gain and loss, honor and dishonor, blame and praise, and pleasure and displeasure arise.

444
00:41:53,336 --> 00:41:55,779
[SPEAKER_05]: And so do they for a learned and noble disciple.

445
00:41:57,071 --> 00:42:02,413
[SPEAKER_05]: What, then, is the difference between a learned noble cycle and an ordinary unlearned person?

446
00:42:02,453 --> 00:42:10,016
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's what we were saying before, like, if it's just about these same mundane things, what is it that you don't understand?

447
00:42:10,977 --> 00:42:19,120
[SPEAKER_05]: And so, it says, because gain arises for an unlearned ordinary person, they don't reflect.

448
00:42:19,800 --> 00:42:21,601
[SPEAKER_05]: This gain has arisen for me.

449
00:42:21,641 --> 00:42:23,722
[SPEAKER_05]: It's impermanent, suffering, imperishable.

450
00:42:24,442 --> 00:42:25,983
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't understand it as it is.

451
00:42:27,043 --> 00:42:28,305
[SPEAKER_05]: Loss arises.

452
00:42:28,325 --> 00:42:33,892
[SPEAKER_05]: Honor, dishonor, blame, praise, displeasure, uh, pleasure, displeasure, uh, arise.

453
00:42:35,074 --> 00:42:36,075
[SPEAKER_05]: Uh, they don't reflect.

454
00:42:36,376 --> 00:42:38,098
[SPEAKER_05]: This displeasure has arisen for me.

455
00:42:38,138 --> 00:42:39,920
[SPEAKER_05]: It's impermanent, suffering, imperishable.

456
00:42:40,441 --> 00:42:41,923
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't understand it as it is.

457
00:42:43,143 --> 00:42:51,348
[SPEAKER_05]: So, gain and loss, honor and dishonor, blame and praise, and pleasure and displeasure, endure with a grip on their mind.

458
00:42:52,248 --> 00:42:55,230
[SPEAKER_05]: They embrace gain and resist loss.

459
00:42:55,630 --> 00:42:58,572
[SPEAKER_05]: They embrace fame and resist disgrace.

460
00:42:59,332 --> 00:43:01,834
[SPEAKER_05]: They embrace praise and oppose blame.

461
00:43:02,014 --> 00:43:03,976
[SPEAKER_05]: They embrace pleasure and oppose displeasure.

462
00:43:05,517 --> 00:43:16,426
[SPEAKER_05]: Being thus engaged in embracing embracing and resisting, they're not freed from birth, aging, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, displeasure, sadness, and distress.

463
00:43:17,046 --> 00:43:18,707
[SPEAKER_05]: They're not freed from suffering, I say.

464
00:43:19,608 --> 00:43:23,590
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, because gain arises for a learned noble disciple, they do reflect.

465
00:43:24,190 --> 00:43:25,551
[SPEAKER_05]: This gain has arisen for me.

466
00:43:25,891 --> 00:43:27,672
[SPEAKER_05]: It's impermanent, suffering, and perishable.

467
00:43:27,992 --> 00:43:29,093
[SPEAKER_05]: They understand it as it is.

468
00:43:30,013 --> 00:43:34,835
[SPEAKER_05]: Loss, honor, dishonor, blame, praise, pleasure, displeasure.

469
00:43:34,895 --> 00:43:35,516
[SPEAKER_05]: They reflect.

470
00:43:35,716 --> 00:43:37,376
[SPEAKER_05]: This pleasure has arisen for me.

471
00:43:37,396 --> 00:43:39,257
[SPEAKER_05]: It's impermanent, suffering, and perishable.

472
00:43:39,938 --> 00:43:41,018
[SPEAKER_05]: They understand it as it is.

473
00:43:41,798 --> 00:43:48,802
[SPEAKER_05]: So, when gain and loss, honor and dishonor, blame and praise, and pleasure and displeasure,

474
00:43:49,911 --> 00:43:52,334
[SPEAKER_05]: Arise, they don't endure with a grip on their mind.

475
00:43:52,835 --> 00:43:56,519
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't embrace gain or resist loss.

476
00:43:56,680 --> 00:44:00,024
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't embrace honor or resist dishonor.

477
00:44:00,184 --> 00:44:03,388
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't embrace praise or resist blame.

478
00:44:03,809 --> 00:44:06,752
[SPEAKER_05]: They don't embrace pleasure or resist displeasure.

479
00:44:07,513 --> 00:44:14,696
[SPEAKER_05]: Having given up embracing and resisting, they're freed from birth, aging, and death, from sorrow, lamentations, pleasure, sadness, and distress.

480
00:44:15,376 --> 00:44:16,957
[SPEAKER_05]: They're freed from suffering, I say.

481
00:44:17,697 --> 00:44:18,697
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, it ends with a...

482
00:44:19,518 --> 00:44:24,459
[SPEAKER_05]: This is the difference between a learned noble disciple and an unlearned ordinary person.

483
00:44:24,519 --> 00:44:25,880
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, it finishes with a verse.

484
00:44:27,738 --> 00:44:39,589
[SPEAKER_05]: Gain and loss, honor and dishonor, blame and praise, and pleasure and displeasure, these qualities among people are impermanent, transient, and perishable.

485
00:44:41,170 --> 00:44:46,335
[SPEAKER_05]: An intelligent and mindful person, having known these things, sees that they're perishable.

486
00:44:47,056 --> 00:44:53,241
[SPEAKER_05]: Pleasing phenomena don't disturb their mind, nor do they harbor resistance against the undesirable.

487
00:44:54,422 --> 00:44:58,465
[SPEAKER_05]: Both embracing and resisting are dispelled and ended.

488
00:44:58,685 --> 00:44:59,485
[SPEAKER_05]: They are no more.

489
00:45:00,286 --> 00:45:06,810
[SPEAKER_05]: Knowing the stainless, sorrowless state, they, gone beyond being, understand rightly.

490
00:45:10,415 --> 00:45:15,080
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the stainless sore of the state being cessation of passion.

491
00:45:15,120 --> 00:45:24,809
[SPEAKER_05]: Cessation of complete cooling down, nibbāna, of passion and aversion and ignorance, in regard to

492
00:45:26,130 --> 00:45:29,174
[SPEAKER_05]: The eight worldly things that you cannot escape from, even if you want to.

493
00:45:29,294 --> 00:45:29,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

494
00:45:29,835 --> 00:45:36,382
[SPEAKER_02]: So, a noble disciple understands the right gain has come to me, but it's impermanent.

495
00:45:36,943 --> 00:45:37,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

496
00:45:37,484 --> 00:45:40,928
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that gain is real, but it's impermanent.

497
00:45:41,108 --> 00:45:41,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

498
00:45:41,849 --> 00:45:42,790
[SPEAKER_02]: He does not say

499
00:45:45,813 --> 00:45:48,614
[SPEAKER_02]: That game is impermanent, therefore it's not real.

500
00:45:49,474 --> 00:45:53,796
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that game is real and it is impermanent and that's precisely what he has understood.

501
00:45:54,316 --> 00:46:02,019
[SPEAKER_02]: So, rather than denying the game, what he sees is the danger in now becoming infatuated with that game, because it's impermanent.

502
00:46:04,800 --> 00:46:12,783
[SPEAKER_05]: See, it's much more, in a sense, mundane, but as you said before, the reason why it's not as attractive

503
00:46:13,632 --> 00:46:18,233
[SPEAKER_05]: It is because it reveals this whole additional dimension of work that you have to do.

504
00:46:18,513 --> 00:46:18,853
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

505
00:46:20,213 --> 00:46:25,214
[SPEAKER_05]: Not things that will just sort themselves out by just seeing them.

506
00:46:25,694 --> 00:46:27,614
[SPEAKER_03]: So, ultimately, the reality is for those who are lazy.

507
00:46:28,595 --> 00:46:30,115
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's your responsibility.

508
00:46:30,595 --> 00:46:31,175
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

509
00:46:31,215 --> 00:46:34,095
[SPEAKER_03]: The responsibility that, yeah, it's... Shame.

510
00:46:34,496 --> 00:46:42,377
[SPEAKER_05]: Not because I'm just not fortunate that I haven't discovered this yet, even though I've been practicing for 20 years.

511
00:46:43,498 --> 00:46:51,191
[SPEAKER_05]: If you're still suffering, it's because, on some level, you must still be fueling the fire of passion.

512
00:46:51,412 --> 00:46:52,894
[SPEAKER_05]: There's no other explanation for it.

